# gitleaks reads this file as a list of finding fingerprints. The entries below
# deliberately omit the leading commit hash: gitleaks matches a fingerprint on
# `file:rule:startline` alone, so these survive a history rewrite, which every
# commit-qualified fingerprint does not. They are still line-numbered, so if one
# of these lines moves the entry must be regenerated:
#
#   gitleaks git --report-format json --report-path leaks.json
#
# then take each finding's `Fingerprint` and drop everything up to the first
# colon. Nothing here suppresses a rule globally; each line names one place.
#
# As of gitleaks 8.30.1 none of these entries actually fires: a scan with this
# file removed reports no findings at all. They are kept as pre-emptive cover, so
# that a later ruleset which does flag these two intentionally public values
# turns CI red for a reason someone has already written down rather than for one
# they have to rediscover. Neither value is a secret; the notes below say why.

# The Supabase publishable key is an intentionally public browser credential.
# Row Level Security is the boundary, not the secrecy of this string.
wrangler.jsonc:generic-api-key:24
wrangler.jsonc:generic-api-key:70

# The stellar.toml route derives and emits only the SEP-10 public key. The
# signing secret is read from the server environment and never rendered. Two
# line numbers because an added import moved the derivation from 8 to 9, and a
# full-history scan still meets the earlier position.
src/routes/.well-known/stellar.toml/+server.ts:generic-api-key:8
src/routes/.well-known/stellar.toml/+server.ts:generic-api-key:9
